    17	proceedings, analysed the relevant studies, and compared and integrated our ﬁndings with the two pre-
    18	vious tertiary studies.Results: We found 67 new SLRs addressing 24 software engineering topics. Among these studies, 15 were
    19	considered relevant to the undergraduate educational curriculum, and 40 appeared of possible interest to
    20	practitioners. We found that the number of SLRs in software engineering is increasing, the overall qualityof the studies is improving, and the number of researchers and research organisations worldwide that are
    21	conducting SLRs is also increasing and spreading.
    22	Conclusion: Our ﬁndings suggest that the software engineering research community is starting to adopt
    23	SLRs consistently as a research method. However, the majority of the SLRs did not evaluate the quality ofprimary studies and fail to provide guidelines for practitioners, thus decreasing their potential impact on
    24	software engineering practice.
