    63	1. Introduction
    64	In 2004, Kitchenham et al. [14] introduced the concept of evi-
    65	dence-based software engineering (EBSE) as a promising approach
    66	to integrate academic research and industrial practice in software
    67	engineering. Following this paper, Dybå et al. [8]presented EBSE
    68	from the point of view of the software engineering practitioner,
    69	and Jørgensen et al. [20] complemented it with an account of the
    70	teaching aspects of EBSE to university students.
    71	By analogy with evidence-based medicine [25], ﬁve steps are
    72	needed to practice EBSE:
    73	1. Convert the need for information (about the practice of soft-
    74	ware engineering) into answerable questions.
    75	2. Identify, with maximum efﬁciency, the best evidence with
    76	which to answer these questions.
    77	3. Appraise the evidence critically: assess its validity (closeness to
    78	the truth) and usefulness (practical applicability).
    79	4. Implement the results of this appraisal in software engineering
    80	practice.
    81	5. Evaluate the performance of this implementation.
    82	The preferred method for implementing Steps 2 and 3 is sys-
    83	tematic literature review (SLR). Kitchenham [15] adapted guide-
    84	lines for performing SLRs in medicine to software engineering.
    85	Later, using concepts from social science [23], Kitchenham and
    86	Charters updated the guidelines [16]. The literature differentiates
    87	several types of systematic reviews [23], including the following:
    88	/C15Conventional SLRs [23], which aggregate results about the effec-
    89	tiveness of a treatment, intervention, or technology, and are
    90	related to speciﬁc research questions such as Is intervention I
    91	on population P more effective for obtaining outcome O in context
    92	C than comparison treatment C? (resulting in the PICOC structure
    93	[23]). When sufﬁcient quantitative experiments are available to
    94	answer the research question, a meta-analysis (MA) can be used
    95	to integrate their results [10].
    96	/C15Mapping (or scoping) studies (MS) [1]aim to identify all
    97	research related to a speciﬁc topic, i.e., to answer broader ques-
    98	tions related to trends in research. Typical questions are explor-
    99	atory, e.g., What do we know about topic T ?
   100	Greenhalgh [11] emphasises that evidence-based practice is not
   101	only about reading papers and summarising their results in a com-
   102	prehensive and unbiased way. It involves reading the right papers
   103	(those both valid and useful) and then changing behaviour in the
   104	practice of the discipline (in our case, software engineering). There-
   105	fore, EBSE is not only about performing high-quality SLRs and mak-
   106	ing them publicly available (Steps 2 and 3). All ﬁve steps should be
   107	performed for a practice to be considered evidence-based. Never-
   108	theless, SLRs can play an important role in supporting research
   109	and education, and informing practice on the impact or effect oftechnology. Therefore, information about how many SLRs are avail-
   110	able in software engineering, where they can be found, which topic
   111	areas have been addressed, and the overall quality of available
   112	studies can greatly beneﬁt the academic community as well as
   113	practitioners.
   114	In this article, we performed a mapping study of SLRs in soft-
   115	ware engineering published between 1st July 2008 and 31st
   116	December 2009. Our goal was to analyse the available secondarystudies and integrate our ﬁndings with the results of the two pre-
   117	vious studies discussed in Section 3. Our work is classiﬁed as a ter-
   118	tiary study because we performed a review of secondary studies.
   119	The study protocol is presented in Section 3. Sections 4 and 5 dis-
   120	cuss the extracted data and their analysis. Finally, the conclusions
   121	are presented in Section 6.
   122	2. Previous studies
   123	Two previous tertiary studies have been performed aiming to
   124	assess the use of SLRs in software engineering research and, indi-
   125	rectly, to investigate the adoption of EBSE by software engineering
   126	researchers [18,19] . Additionally, our team recently performed a
   127	critical appraisal of the SLRs reported in these two studies with re-
   128	spect to the types of research questions asked in published reviews
   129	[7]. In this section, we brieﬂy describe these three studies and their
   130	relationships.
   131	The ﬁrst study, developed by Kitchenham et al. [18] (hereinafter
   132	called the Original Study; OS), found 20 unique studies reporting
   133	literature reviews that were considered systematic according to
   134	the authors. The study utilised a manual search of speciﬁc confer-
   135	ence proceedings and journal papers for peer-reviewed articles
   136	published between 1st January 2004 and 30th June 2008. The OS
   137	identiﬁed several problems or limitations of the existing SLRs, as
   138	follows:
   139	/C15A relatively large number of studies (40%, 8/20) investigated
   140	research methods or trends rather than performing technique
   141	evaluation, which should be the focus of a (conventional) sys-
   142	tematic review [23].
   143	/C15The diversity of software engineering topics was limited: seven
   144	related to cost estimation, three related to test methods, and
   145	three related to software engineering experiments.
   146	/C15The number of primary studies cited was much larger in the
   147	mapping studies than in the SLRs.
   148	/C15Relatively few SLRs assessed the quality of the primary studies.
   149	/C15Relatively few papers provided advice oriented towards
   150	practitioners.
   151	The last two problems identiﬁed above are of the most concern,
   152	as the stated purpose of evidence-based practice is to inform and
   153	advise practitioners on (high-quality) empirical evidence that can
   154	be used to improve their practice. We investigated whether these
   155	problems persist.900 F.Q.B. da Silva et al. / Information and Software Technology 53 (2011) 899–913
   156
   157	--- Page 3 ---
   158	One limitation of the OS was that the search was manual and
   159	performed on a relevant but restricted set of sources. Therefore,
   160	relevant studies may have been missed, as was, in fact, the case
   161	according to the ﬁndings of the second tertiary study performed
   162	by Kitchenham et al. [19]. This study (hereinafter called the First
   163	Extension Study; FE) used an automatic search on ﬁve search en-
   164	gines and indexing systems and found 33 additional unique studies
   165	published between 1st January 2004 and 30th June 2008. The FE
   166	identiﬁed some improvements on the issues found in the OS; the
   167	number of SLRs was increasing, as was the overall quality of the
   168	studies. However, the problem remained that only a few SLRs fol-
   169	lowed a speciﬁc methodology, included practitioner guidelines, or
   170	evaluated the quality of primary studies. The authors also empha-
   171	sised that researchers from the USA, the leading country in soft-
   172	ware engineering research, authored a very small number of SLRsand this could be interpreted as a sign of limitation on the adoption
   173	of evidence-based software engineering or that this adoption is
   174	mainly concentrated in European research groups.
   175	Our group [7]subsequently reported the results of a study per-
   176	formed on the 53 SLRs presented in the OS and FE with the goal of
   177	assessing the SLRs with respect to the types of research questions
   178	asked in the selected reviews, how the questions were presented in
   179	the reports, and how the questions were used to guide the search
   180	of the primary studies. We found that over 65% of the research
   181	questions asked in the 53 reviews were exploratory and that only
   182	15% investigated causality questions. Additionally, we found that
   183	half of the studies did not state their research questions explicitly,
   184	and for those that did, 75% did not use the questions to explicitly
   185	guide the search for primary studies.
   186	In our study, we also compared the types of research questions
   187	asked and the classiﬁcation of the studies presented in the OS and
   188	FE studies as systematic reviews or mapping studies. To classify
   189	the research questions, we used the terminology deﬁned by Easter-
   190	brook et al. [9]. We found that most studies classiﬁed as mapping
   191	studies in OS and FE asked exploratory questions, and the vast
   192	majority of the studies that asked causal or relational questions
   193	were classiﬁed as (conventional) systematic reviews. Using these
   194	results, we adopted a systematic approach to classify the reviews
   195	as mapping studies or systematic reviews based on their types of
   196	research questions; the same approach was used in the current
   197	study to classify the reviews and thus enable the comparison of
   198	our results with those of the OS and FE.
   199	3. Method
   200	The research group that developed the OS and FE reviews in-
   201	tended to repeat their study at the end of 2009 to ‘‘track the progressof SLRs and evidence-based software engineering’’ [18]. During the
   202	14th International Conference on Evaluation and Assessment in
   203	Software Engineering (EASE’2010) in May 2010, at Keele University,
   204	we discussed this extension with two members of the group, Pearl
   205	Brereton and David Budgen, and they said that the extension had
   206	not yet been performed. We informed them of our intention of per-
   207	forming this extension and integrating the results with the OS and
   208	FE ﬁndings. At that meeting, two methodological decisions were
   209	made. First, our extension would be performed independently of
   210	their work and with as little exchange of information as possible.
   211	Second, we would use, as closely as possible, the same protocol used
   212	in the FE. These decisions would assure that one study would not
   213	inﬂuence or bias the other, and we would be able to compare the re-
   214	sults, as the protocols would be the same. Therefore, the method
   215	used in our study follows closely the protocol deﬁned by Kitchen-
   216	ham et al. [17] and the structure used by Kitchenham et al. [19].
   217	Hereinafter, we use OS/FE to refer to the combination of the re-
   218	sults related to the 53 SLRs from the OS (20) and FE (33). Finally,we use OS/FE + SE to refer to the combination of results of OS/FE
   219	with our ﬁndings, based on 67 SLRs, representing a total of 120 sec-
   220	ondary studies.
   221	3.1. Research questions
   222	The ﬁve research questions (RQ) investigated in the SE were
   223	equivalent to the research questions used in the FE [19]. We per-
   224	formed minor adjustments and added subquestions as follows.
   225	RQ1: How many SLRs were published between 1st January 2004
   226	and 31st December 2009?
   227	RQ1.1: How many SLRs were published between 1st January
   228	2004 and 30th June 2008?
   229	RQ1.2: How many SLRs were published between 1st July 2008
   230	and 31st December 2009?
   231	The subquestions of RQ1 investigate the development of SLRs in
   232	two separate periods. To answer RQ1.1, we used the results of OS/
   233	FE[18,19] , whereas for RQ1.2, we performed the processes of
   234	search, selection, quality assessment, and data extraction deﬁned
   235	in Sections 3.3–3.5 . Similarly, we addressed the next questions
   236	considering the two time periods as we explicitly did for RQ1,
   237	searching for new evidence, combining with the results of the pre-
   238	vious studies, and integrating all ﬁndings.
   239	RQ2: What research topics are being addressed?
   240	RQ3: Which individuals and organisations are most active in
   241	SLR-based research?
   242	The fourth question in the FE was, ‘‘Are the limitations of SLRs,
   243	as observed in the original study, still an issue?’’ We changed the
   244	fourth question to the following:
   245	RQ4: Are the limitations of SLRs, as observed in the two previ-
   246	ous studies, FE and OS, still an issue?
   247	Finally, we kept the ﬁfth question unaltered:
   248	RQ5: Is the quality of SLRs improving?
   249	3.2. Research team
   250	A team of six researchers, who co-authored this article, devel-
   251	oped this study. Three of them, Fabio Silva, André Santos, and Sér-
   252	gio Soares (referred to as R1,R2, and R3, respectively) are full-time
   253	lecturers. Cleviton Monteiro, and César França ( R4and R5) are Ph.D.
   254	students, and Felipe Farias ( R6) is a Master’s student. All research-
   255	ers are afﬁliated with the Centre for Informatics, Federal University
   256	of Pernambuco, Brazil.
   257	3.3. Decision procedure
   258	Three important activities in a systematic review require deci-
   259	sions about possibly conﬂicting situations: study selection, quality
   260	evaluation, and data extraction. It is thus recommended that such
   261	activities be performed by at least two researchers. Therefore, a
   262	process to support decision-making and consensus is necessary.
   263	For this study, we deﬁned a decision and consensus procedure
   264	(DCP), shown in Fig. 1 .
   265	The procedure starts with a list of unevaluated studies as the in-
   266	put for a decision process (study selection, quality assessment, or
   267	data extraction), which R1randomly allocates to two researchers
   268	(Riand Rj). After individual evaluation, the results ( riand rj) are
   269	integrated by R4and R5into an Agreement/Disagreement Table
   270	(ADT). Next, R1randomly allocates the results from the ADT toF.Q.B. da Silva et al. / Information and Software Technology 53 (2011) 899–913 901
   271
   272	--- Page 4 ---
   273	the researchers, making sure that a different researcher ( Rk) evalu-
   274	ates the results. The students do not participate in this stage. Rk
   275	judges the disagreements in the ADT and produces one of three re-
   276	sults: an agreement for one of the previous decisions, a third result
   277	(rk), or retention of the original disagreement. The remaining dis-
   278	agreements are resolved by a consensus of all six researchers. After
   279	consensus is reached, all results are integrated by R4and R5into the
   280	ﬁnal list of evaluated studies.

