   281	3.4. Search process
   282	We performed our search for peer-reviewed articles published
   283	between 1st July 2008 and 31st December 2009. Differing from
   284	OS and FE, we combined automatic and manual searches to in-
   285	crease coverage. The automatic search was performed by R4and
   286	R5on six search engines and indexing systems: ACM Digital Li-
   287	brary, IEEEXplore Digital Library, Science Direct, CiteSeerX, ISI
   288	Web of Science, and Scopus. All searches were performed on the
   289	entire paper, including title and abstract, except for the ISI Web
   290	of Science, where the search was based only on title and topic
   291	due to limitations imposed by the search engine. This is the string
   292	used in the automatic search:
   293	(‘‘software engineering’’) AND (‘‘review of studies’’ OR ‘‘structured
   294	review’’ OR ‘‘systematic review’’ OR ‘‘literature review’’ OR ‘‘litera-
   295	ture analysis’’ OR ‘‘in-depth survey’’ OR ‘‘literature survey’’ OR
   296	‘‘meta analysis’’ OR ‘‘past studies’’ OR ‘‘subject matter expert’’ OR
   297	‘‘analysis of research’’ OR ‘‘empirical body of knowledge’’ OR ‘‘over-
   298	view of existing research’’ OR ‘‘body of published research’’ OR ‘‘evi-
   299	dence-based’’ OR ‘‘evidence based’’ OR ‘‘study synthesis’’ OR ‘‘study
   300	aggregation’’) .
   301	The syntax was the same for all engines, except for the ISI Web
   302	of Science, which required minor syntax changes due to the char-
   303	acteristics of the engine. The semantics of the strings remained un-
   304	changed. The search process was validated against the papers
   305	found in the OS and FE studies. Only three papers were not found
   306	by our search process. The study by Barcelos and Travassos [3]was
