   383
   384	--- Page 5 ---
   385	articles were selected for quality assessment and data extraction
   386	(see Fig. 2 ).
   387	3.6. Quality assessment
   388	The OS and FE studies assessed the quality of the SLRs using the
   389	set of criteria deﬁned by the Centre for Reviews and Dissemination
   390	(CDR) Database of Abstracts of Reviews of Effects (DARE) of York
   391	University [5]. This version of the DARE criteria was based on four
   392	questions:
   393	/C15QA1: Are the review’s inclusion and exclusion criteria described
   394	and appropriate?
   395	/C15QA2: Is the literature search likely to have covered all relevant
   396	studies?
   397	/C15QA3: Did the reviewers assess the quality/validity of the
   398	included studies?
   399	/C15QA4: Were the basic data/studies adequately described?
   400	The same scoring procedure used by Kitchenham et al. [19] was
   401	used in our study to assign scores to each question, which were
   402	then summed to yield the ﬁnal quality score of the review. The an-
   403	swers for the quality assessment questions were obtained using
   404	the following criteria (the criteria for QA2 was modiﬁed from the
   405	original version used by Kitchenham et al. [19] to solve an ambigu-
   406	ity related to the use of automated search; the modiﬁcations are
   407	marked in bold face):
   408	/C15QA1: Y (yes), the inclusion criteria are explicitly deﬁned in the
   409	review; P (Partly), the inclusion criteria are implicit; N (no),
   410	the inclusion criteria are not deﬁned and cannot be readily
   411	inferred.
   412	/C15QA2: Y (yes), the authors have either searched four or more dig-
   413	ital libraries and included additional search strategies or identi-
   414	ﬁed and referenced all journals addressing the topic of interest;
   415	P (Partly), the authors have searched four digital libraries with
   416	no extra search strategies or three digital libraries (regard-
   417	less of the use of extra search strategies) , or searched a
   418	deﬁned but restricted set of journals and conference proceed-
   419	ings; N, the authors have searched up to two digital libraries
   420	(regardless of the use of extra search strategies) or an extre-
   421	mely restricted set of journals.1
   422	/C15QA3: Y (yes), the authors have explicitly deﬁned the quality cri-
   423	teria and extracted them from each primary study; P (Partly),
   424	the research question involves quality issues that are addressed
   425	by the study; N (No), no explicit quality assessment of individ-ual primary study has been attempted or quality data has been
   426	extracted but not used.
   427	/C15QA4: Y (Yes), information is presented about each primary
   428	study so that the data summaries can clearly be traced to rele-
   429	vant studies; P (Partly), only summary information is presented
   430	about individual studies, e.g., studies are grouped into catego-
   431	ries but it is not possible to link individual studies to each cat-
   432	egory; N (No), the results of the individual studies are not
   433	speciﬁed, i.e., the individual primary studies are not cited.
   434	The scoring procedure was Y = 1, P = 0.5, and N = 0, as per-
   435	formed by Kitchenham et al. [19]. In the planning stage of our
   436	study, we noticed that the DARE criteria have changed and the cur-
   437	rent version has ﬁve questions [6]. Despite this change, we used
   438	the same quality criteria as OS and FE to allow for comparabilityof the results. The DCP was also used in the quality assessment
   439	producing the quality scores for all 77 papers.
   440	To verify the consistency of our quality assessment with the one
   441	performed by Kitchenham et al. [19], we performed a blind assess-
   442	ment of 10 SLRs from the SE study and compared our scores with
   443	the results of Kitchenham et al. [19]. We agreed on all scores of
   444	eight studies, and disagreed on only one criterion in each of the
   445	two remaining studies, which was considered a very good agree-
   446	ment. Therefore, we are conﬁdent that our quality assessment
   447	can be compared to that presented by Kitchenham et al. [19].
   448	3.7. Data extraction process
   449	We extracted the following data from the 77 studies to answer
   450	the research questions:
   451	/C15TheYear of publication.
   452	/C15TheQuality Score of the study.
   453	/C15The Review Type , related to whether the study is a conven-
   454	tional systematic literature review (SLR), a meta-analysis (MA)
   455	or a mapping study (MS).
   456	/C15TheReview Scope , related to the whether the study focused on
   457	a detailed technical question (RQ), on (research) trends in a par-
   458	ticular software engineering topic area (SERT), or on research
   459	methods in software engineering (RT).
   460	/C15The software engineering Topic Area addressed by the study.
   461	/C15Whether the study explicitly Cited EBSE papers ([14,8,20] )o r
   462	Cited Guidelines ([15,16] ).
   463	/C15TheNumber of Primary studies analysed in the SLR, as stated
   464	in the paper either explicitly or as part of tabulations.
   465	/C15Whether the study Included Practitioners Guidelines explic-
   466	itly as an identiﬁable part (section, table, etc.) of the paper.
   467	/C15TheSource Type in which the study was ﬁrst reported (J = jour-
   468	nal, C = Conference, WS = Workshop, BS = Book Series).
   469	After analysing the results of the data extraction, we decided to
   470	exclude 10 studies: four were not on Software Engineering, three
   471	were reports of the results of two SLRs that appeared in the FE study,
   472	one was from 2010 (outside of the time period of this study), one
   473	was a shorter version of [SE01] published in another journal, and
   474	one received zero in the quality evaluation and did not have mostof the required information. The DCP was used for data extraction,
   475	and at the end of this process, 67 articles were selected for further
   476	analysis and answering the research questions (see Appendix A).
   477	4. Data extraction results
   478	A summary of the data collected from the 67 SLRs in the above
   479	processes is shown in Table 2 . Regarding the nature of the
   480	references to the EBSE papers and SLR Guidelines, similarly to theTable 1
