   488	--- Page 6 ---
   489	ﬁndings reported by Kitchenham et al. [18,19] , all papers that cited
   490	the EBSE papers or the guidelines did so as a methodological justi-
   491	ﬁcation for their study, so we considered all SLRs to be EBSE-
   492	positioned.
   493	Table 3 shows the quality scores for each assessment question.
   494	We ordered the studies by the ﬁnal score and divided the set into
   495	quartiles to allow for easier visualisation of how the entire set of
   496	studies performed in the assessment. The implications of the qual-
   497	ity assessment results are discussed in Section 5.5.
   498	5. Discussion of research questions
   499	In this section, we address the research questions presented in
   500	Section 3.1. We show the results of our study (SE), compare them
   501	with the ﬁndings of OS/FE, and integrate the results (OS/FE + SE).
   502	5.1. RQ1: How many SLRs were published between 1st January 2004
   503	and 31st December 2009?
   504	Table 4 shows the growth in published SLRs since 2004. The OS/
   505	FE studies found 53 studies between 2004 and June 2008(4.5 years), and our extension (SE) found 67 studies between July
   506	2008 and December 2009 (1.5 years). The studies published in
   507	2009 account for 43% (51/120) of the total.
   508	Table 4 also shows that the number of SLRs that cite either the
   509	EBSE papers or the SLR guidelines also increased in absolute num-
   510	ber and also as a percentage of the studies in a given year. In fact, in
   511	the SE, 80% (53/67) of the SLRs cited the EBSE paper, the SLR Guide-
   512	lines, or both.
   513	5.2. RQ2: What research topics are being addressed?
   514	As shown in Table 2 , the 67 reviews in SE addressed 24 different
   515	software engineering topics, 14 of which were not addressed in the
   516	OS/FE. The most frequent topics in our SLRs were the following:
   517	Requirements Engineering (eight studies), Distributed Software
   518	Development (8), Software Product Line (7), Software Testing (6),
   519	Empirical Research Methods (5), Software Maintenance and Evalu-
   520	ation, and Agile Software Development (4).
   521	To evaluate the coverage of software engineering topics, we
   522	used the same rationale used by Kitchenham et al. [19], and there-
