  1026	explicit quality evaluation was still very low, amounting to only
  1027	21% (14/67) of the reviews in the SE study.
  1028	Three situations that might explain the low percentage of qual-
  1029	ity assessment of the primary reviews were observed. First, some
  1030	researchers appear to have confused quality assessment with
  1031	explicitly stating the inclusion/exclusion criteria of the primary
  1032	studies and may therefore have thought that no further quality
  1033	assessment was necessary (e.g., [SE42] ). Second, in some cases,
  1034	quality assessment was thought to be unnecessary because the pri-
  1035	mary studies were retrieved from ‘‘trustworthy sources’’ (e.g.,
  1036	peer-reviewed journals), and this was considered to be sufﬁcient
  1037	to guarantee the quality of the primary studies (e.g., [SE43] ). Third,in other cases, the search process found so few relevant studies
  1038	that the researchers may have feared that applying quality criteria
  1039	would leave them with no studies to analyse (e.g., [SE39] ).
  1040	Researchers performing systematic reviews should attempt to ad-
  1041	dress these situations, as none are acceptable explanations for not
  1042	performing quality assessment.
