  1201	--- Page 13 ---
  1202	We then compared the mean of the quality scores of the SLR
  1203	with respect to three other factors. First, the SLRs that explicitly
  1204	provided guidelines for practitioners had higher mean quality
  1205	scores (Mean = 2.85, r= 0.91) than those that did not provide
  1206	guidelines (Mean = 2.38, r= 0.83). Second, SLRs published in Jour-
  1207	nals had higher quality scores (Mean = 2.69, r= 0.94) than the
  1208	studies published in Conferences (Mean = 2.44, r= 0.81). Third,
  1209	the SLRs with scope RQ performed better (Mean = 2.88, r= 0.76),
  1210	than those with SERT (Mean = 2.41, r= 0.91) and RT (Mean = 2.28,
  1211	r= 0.79). We performed a regression analysis using these three
  1212	factors, and the result was statistically signiﬁcant for a 95% conﬁ-dence level, as follows: Guidelines for Practitioners ( B= 0.183,
  1213	std. error = 0.038, p= 0.000), Journal ( B= 0.117, std. error = 0.041,
  1214	p= 0.005) and RQ ( B= 0.081, std. error = 0.036, p= 0.025).
  1215	Finally, we correlated the number of primary studies in an SLR
  1216	with the quality score using Pearson’s coefﬁcient and found that
  1217	the inverse correlation was signiﬁcant ( r=/C00.204, N= 120,
  1218	p= 0.05). As reported by Kitchenham et al. [19], SLRs addressing
  1219	larger numbers of primary studies had lower quality scores than
  1220	those with fewer primary studies. A possible explanation is that
  1221	when faced with too many studies to analyse, researchers may
  1222	opt not to perform quality assessment, and they may also have
  1223	more difﬁculties in presenting a good synthesis and summary of
  1224	evidence for each paper, thus scoring lower on quality questions
  1225	QA3 and QA4.
  1226	6. Limitations of this study
  1227	Two major problems in SLRs are ﬁnding all the relevant studies
  1228	and assessing their quality. In our study, we employed a mixed
  1229	process approach to ﬁnd relevant studies that combined an auto-
  1230	matic search in search engines, manual search on relevant journalsand conference proceedings, and backward search, that is, search-
  1231	ing for relevant studies in the references of previously selected
  1232	studies. We checked the coverage of our automatic search and only
  1233	failed to recover one study in a set of 51, which can be considered
  1234	good coverage if the automatic search is complemented by manual
  1235	procedures.
  1236	Quality assessment of the SLRs was performed by at least two
  1237	researchers, and conﬂicts were resolved by a third researcher or
  1238	by consensus in cases in which the third point of view was also
  1239	conﬂicting. This multi-evaluator procedure increased our conﬁ-
  1240	dence on the reliability of our quality assessment. However, we
  1241	found the scoring procedure to be too subjective for question
  1242	QA4 and inconsistent for QA2. We solved the inconsistency prob-
  1243	lem by consulting the researchers that performed the OS/FE stud-
  1244	ies. The problems with question QA4 caused many disagreementsbetween evaluators that were only solved in the consensus meeting.
  1245	Two researchers, following the process described in Fig. 1 of
  1246	Section 3.2, performed data extraction independently. Neverthe-
  1247	less, on many occasions, we found that quality assessment and
  1248	data extraction may have been compromised by the way most of
  1249	the SLRs were reported. Many reviews did not present sufﬁcient
  1250	information, or the organisation of the presentation made it very
  1251	difﬁcult to locate the needed information in the extraction process.
  1252	More speciﬁcally, we often found that review protocols were not
  1253	described in sufﬁcient detail, in particular, regarding the search
  1254	process and quality assessment of the primary studies. In many
  1255	cases, information was not explicitly stated in the reports and
  1256	had to be inferred from the text. Despite our efforts to reach con-
  1257	sensus during data extraction and quality assessment, it is possible
  1258	that the extraction and quality assessment processes may have re-
  1259	sulted in inaccurate data.
  1260	7. Conclusions
  1261	This tertiary study analysed 1455 articles, of which 67 were
  1262	considered to be systematic literature reviews in software engi-
  1263	neering with acceptable quality and relevance. Among these stud-
  1264	ies, 15 appeared relevant to the undergraduate educational
  1265	curriculum, 40 appeared of possible interest to practitioners, and
  1266	26 were directed mainly to researchers. Furthermore, the 67 stud-
  1267	ies addressed 24 different software engineering topics, covering
  1268	33% (15/46) of the SWEBOK sections.
  1269	Our study shows three important changes in the study set from
  1270	the previous tertiary studies [18,19] . First, the coverage of topics in
  1271	software engineering increased, and the concentration in a few
  1272	topics decreased. Second, the number of researchers and, conse-
  1273	quently, organisations undertaking systematic reviews increased
  1274	and became more globally distributed. Finally, we found propor-tionally more mapping studies than conventional systematic re-
  1275	views in our study. Together, these results appear to indicate
  1276	that systematic reviews are increasingly being considered as an
  1277	important tool in performing unbiased and comprehensive litera-
  1278	ture mappings of the research in speciﬁc topics in software
  1279	engineering.
  1280	However, our ﬁndings also show three major limitations with
  1281	the current use of SLRs in software engineering. First, a large num-
  1282	ber of SLRs still do not assess the quality of their primary studies,
  1283	and this is consistent with the ﬁndings in the previous tertiary
  1284	studies. Second, the integration of the results of the primary stud-
  1285	ies was poorly conducted by many SLRs. In the set of 67 reviews,only one ( [SE37] ) used a meta-analysis [10] to synthesise quantita-
  1286	tive studies and two ( [SE05,SE68] ) used meta-ethnography [21] in
  1287	the synthesis of qualitative studies. Although meta-analysis is
  1288	mentioned in other studies [SE01,SE19,SE22,SE28,SE35,SE56,SE71] ,
  1289	they did not employ the technique. Apart from meta-analysis and
  1290	meta-ethnography, no other form of meta-synthesis [26] was used.Table 11
