   666	this way, our ﬁndings can be directly compared to those found
   667	by Kitchenham et al. [19].I nTable 5 , the relationships between
   668	the SLRs and the SE Curriculum and the SWEBOK are shown. To
   669	make the presentation clearer, we omitted from this table the SLRs
   670	that are only of interest to academics.
   671	InTable 6 , the distribution of SLR topics over the 2004 SE Cur-
   672	riculum and the SWEBOK are shown and compared to the ﬁndings
   673	of Kitchenham et al. [19]. This comparison shows that the coverage
   674	has increased but it is still very sparse for both the SE Curriculumand the SWEBOK; it also shows that the intersection between the
   675	previous OS/FE studies and the SE is very low, meaning that the
   676	more recent reviews addressed new topics. Finally, Software Con-
   677	ﬁguration Management and Software Quality were not addressed
   678	by any of the 120 SLRs in the OS/FE + SE set of studies.
   679	5.3. RQ3: Which individuals and organisations are most active in SLR-
   680	based research?
   681	In the OS, a single researcher, Magne Jørgensen, from the Simula
   682	Lab, Norway, who was involved in eight studies, dominated the SLR
   683	publications. At the organisational level, Simula researchers con-
   684	tributed to 11 studies, just over half of the total. The FE showeda trend of reducing this concentration, as more researchers from
   685	different organisations and from other parts of the world began
   686	to adopt SLRs as a research method. In total, 103 researchers from
   687	17 countries and 46 organisations were involved in the develop-
   688	ment of SLRs in the OS/FE.
   689	In our study, the trend of the reduction in the concentrations of
   690	researchers, organisations, and countries was observed to con-
   691	tinue. The number of researchers grew to 159, representing a
   692	50% increase, which appears high when we consider that there
   693	was a 26% increase in the number of SLRs compared to the OS/
   694	FE. This indicates that the number of authors per study increased.
   695	In fact, we found that the percentage of studies co-authored by 3 or
   696	more researchers grew from 58% (31/53) to 67% (45/67), and the
   697	percentage of studies authored by a single researcher fell from
   698	13% (7/53) to 1% (1/67). This indicates an evolution, as the SLR
   699	guidelines and literature emphasise the need for at least two
   700	researchers to perform an SLR to assure higher levels of quality, re-
   701	duce bias, and increase the reliability of the results.Another ﬁnding is that the number of researchers involved in
   702	more than one SLR also increased. In the OS, only ﬁve researchers
   703	were involved in more than three studies; in the FE, another seven
   704	researchers co-authored three or more studies, and in the SE, 10
   705	new researchers entered this group. Furthermore, considering the
   706	OS/FE + SE dataset, there were 24 researchers who co-authored
   707	two studies, and 125 were involved in only one study. Table 7 lists
   708	the 21 researchers that have co-authored three or more SLRs since
   709	2004. This is an indication that, at least for this group of research-
   710	ers, the use of SLRs has gone from being a one-off activity to be-
   711	come part of the research methods regularly employed by these
   712	researchers.
   713	In terms of afﬁliations, in the SE, we found 55 organisations
   714	with researchers involved in developing SLRs, which, combined
   715	with the 43 organisations from the OS/FE, total 90 distinct organi-
   716	sations involved since 2004. The countries in which these organisa-
   717	tions are located also increased in number and become more
   718	widely distributed among the various regions of the world. The
   719	number of countries grew from 17 in the OS/FE to 25 in the OS/
   720	FE + SE, with eight new countries in the SE study. Asian countries,
   721	which did not appear in the OS/FE, contributed to 10 studies 15%
   722	(10/67) in the SE. Only 2 countries that appeared in the OS/FE were
   723	not found in the SE (Israel and Colombia). As shown in Table 8 ,
   724	researchers afﬁliated with European organisations still performed
   725	the vast majority of studies, a trend that remains since the OS.
   726	The participation of USA researchers can still be considered low,
   727	accounting for fewer than 12% (14/120) of the studies.
   728	Altogether, this seems to indicate that SLRs are becoming more
   729	widespread in the scientiﬁc community. More researchers are
   730	using SLRs as a research method, and this use is spreading beyond
   731	Europe, where the majority of the promoters of EBSE and System-
   732	atic Reviews reside.
   733	5.4. RQ4: Are the limitations of SLRs, as observed in the two previous
   734	studies, FE and OS, still an issue?
   735	Some limitations of the SLRs identiﬁed in the OS and FE are dis-
   736	cussed in this section.
   737	5.4.1. Review topics and extent of evidence
   738	As discussed in Section 5.2, the number of topics in software
   739	engineering covered by SLR and MS has increased since the OS
   740	and FE studies. There is no longer a concentration on a single topicTable 2 (continued )
