   774	(Software Cost Estimation), but there is still a concentration on six
   775	topics that were addressed by 55% of the reviews: Empirical Re-
   776	search Methods (16 studies), Software Cost (13), Requirements
   777	Engineering (10), Distributed Software Development (9), SoftwareDevelopment (in general) (9), Software Testing (9), and Software
   778	Maintenance and Evolution (7).
   779	The FE reported a reduction on the proportion of studies focus-
   780	ing on research methods between those in the OS (40%) and the
   781	new studies found in the FE (18%). In the SE, this trend was not ob-
   782	served, as we identiﬁed 27% of the studies (18/67) as being focused
   783	on research methods or primarily aimed at researchers. In the com-
   784	bined dataset of the OS/FE and our study, reviews of Empirical Re-
   785	search Methods are the most frequent topic of study, being
   786	addressed by over 13% (16/120) of the SLRs.
   787	Consistent with the OS and FE studies, mapping studies (MS)
   788	analysed more primary studies than conventional systematic re-
   789	views ( Table 9 ).
   790	We found proportionally more MSs (82%, 55/67) than in the
   791	combined OS/FE data (32%, 17/53). Conversely, we found propor-
   792	tionally fewer conventional SLRs (18%, 12/67) than in the OS/FE
   793	(68%, 36/53). Two reasons may account for this difference. First,
   794	we classiﬁed the studies using the method presented by Da Silva
   795	et al. [7], and the researchers in the OS and FE used an unreported
   796	method. In fact, using the results of Da Silva et al. [7], the propor-
   797	tions in the OS/FE studies changed to 72% MS and 38% SLR, much
   798	closer to the proportions found in the SE. Moreover, the OS study
   799	did not distinguish between MSs and SLRs, classifying all studies
   800	as SLR, which increased the number of SLRs in the OS/FE studies.
   801	Second, as described in Section 5.3, we found that an increasing
   802	number of newcomers (59), that is, researchers performing a sys-
   803	tematic review for the ﬁrst time, published reviews in new topic
   804	areas. Performing an MS of a topic area is a natural ﬁrst step in re-
   805	search, especially if the area is more recently developed, for in-
   806	stance, agile development or distributed software development.
   807	5.4.2. Orientation towards the practice
   808	Twenty reviews in the SE study addressed research questions of
   809	possible interest to practitioners, including 11 that directly ad-
   810	dressed technical evaluation questions (RQ). Furthermore, 36%
   811	(24/67) of the SE studies provided guidelines for practitioners,
   812	either explicitly or implicitly. These ﬁgures show an increase in
   813	the number of SLRs providing practitioner guidelines in compari-
   814	son with the OS and FE studies, showing an increase in the orien-
   815	tation of the SLRs towards the practice of software engineering
   816	(Table 10 ).
   817	However, 58% (39/67) of the reviews in the SE addressed trends
   818	in software engineering research that are only of indirect interest
   819	to practitioners, and eight studies investigated research methods
   820	of no interest in practice.
