  1324	--- Page 14 ---
  1325	We believe that the underlying problem is that the studies we ana-
  1326	lysed, in particular the mapping studies, attempted to combine and
  1327	synthesise results from too diverse a set of primary studies without
  1328	the use of a proper methodology.
  1329	Third, as was identiﬁed in the OS/FE studies, the number of SLRs
  1330	providing guidelines to practitioners remains small. Furthermore,
  1331	we could not identify from the reported data in the SLRs whether
  1332	the investigated question originated in the industrial practice of
  1333	software engineering or as an academic problem. Because the prac-
  1334	tical origin of the problem and practitioner guidelines are essential
  1335	for developing Steps 1, 4, and 5 of the EBSE approach discussed in
  1336	Section 1, we concluded that EBSE is not being fully realised in
  1337	practice. Nevertheless, the number of SLRs is increasing, along with
  1338	the number of researchers and organisations performing them.
  1339	This may indicate that SLRs are being adopted as a research meth-od for the discovery of gaps and trends that could guide academic
  1340	research in software engineering. This was corroborated by the in-
  1341	crease in the proportion of mapping studies, as these are typically
  1342	directed towards exploratory investigation of research trends.
  1343	A systematic review can be updated and extended in at least
  1344	three complementary ways. A temporal update can be performed
  1345	to expand the timeframe for the publication of the primary studies,
  1346	without major changes in the original review protocol. This tertiary
  1347	study is an example of a temporal update of a previous review [19].
  1348	A search extension can be performed to expand the number of
  1349	sources and the search strategies (manual or automated) within
  1350	the same timeframe as the original review to increase the coverage
  1351	of the original study. The tertiary study performed by Kitchenham
  1352	et al. [19] was a search extension of a previous study [18]
  1353	(although it also increased the timeframe of the search). Finally,
  1354	a temporal update and search extension can be combined. We
  1355	found neither an update nor an extension of a previous SLR among
  1356	the 120 studies. We believe that producing updates and extensions
  1357	with proper integration of the ﬁndings with the original reviews is
  1358	an important research activity, and more researchers should en-
  1359	gage in such studies. In particular, we believe that external updates
  1360	or extensions, in the sense of being performed by different
  1361	researchers than the original authors, is important in detecting
  1362	possible bias or imprecision in data extraction and analysis that
  1363	may have been introduced by the original researchers.
  1364	As described in Section 6, we faced difﬁculties during quality
  1365	assessment and data extraction due to the way various SLRs were
  1366	reported. There was very little consistency in the way the articles
  1367	are organised, including the use of section headings to indicate
  1368	important parts or stages of the reviews. Additionally, many SLRs
  1369	omitted essential data, including important parts of the review
  1370	protocol, and often, the same information was presented inconsis-
  1371	tently in different parts of the article. We believe that researchersperforming and reporting systematic reviews would beneﬁt from
  1372	reading reviews that are organised to enhance readability and al-
  1373	low for better data extraction, assessment, and comparison among
  1374	studies. We found a few reviews that provide good examples of
  1375	organisation and content, including SE01,SE02,SE05,SE35,SE55] .
  1376	In future work, we will investigate the extent to which the EBSE
  1377	is being realised regarding the development of all steps deﬁned in
  1378	Section 1. One research approach will be to conduct a broad ﬁeld
  1379	survey with the researchers involved in the 120 SLRs to investigate
  1380	the origins and motivations of their questions and the application
  1381	of the results of their SLRs. We also plan to perform continual up-
  1382	dates to this tertiary study on at least a yearly basis. Finally, we be-
  1383	gan to investigate the methods used by the researchers to integrate
  1384	qualitative data. This is relevant due to the increasing incidence of
  1385	qualitative research in software engineering. We expect to pro-
  1386	duce, from the best approaches of qualitative data analysis em-
  1387	ployed, guidelines for researchers performing SLRs of qualitative
  1388	studies, as has been done in other ﬁelds [26].Acknowledgements
